{"type": "FeatureCollection", "features": [{"type": "Feature", "properties": {"company": "Amtrak", "line": "Amtrak: National Limited", "id": "amtrak-national-limited-historical", "color": "#1c4e80", "mode": "intercity_rail", "avg_speed_kmh": 75, "speed_source": "estimated, consistent with contemporary Amtrak long-distance schedules; unsourced", "segments": [{"start": 1971, "end": 1979, "headway_min": 1440, "note": "Began May 1, 1971 - Amtrak's first day - linking New York and Washington (splitting/joining at Harrisburg) with St. Louis and Kansas City. Initially combined with the Broadway Limited as far as Pittsburgh; split into its own train there from July 12, 1971. West of Pittsburgh it used the ex-Pennsylvania Railroad 'Panhandle Route' (named for cutting through West Virginia's northern panhandle near Steubenville) via Columbus, Dayton, and Indianapolis to St. Louis, then ex-Missouri Pacific trackage on to Kansas City. Despite reusing a name and a general endpoint pair from a B&O train that ran 1925-1971, this Amtrak train shared no trackage with its B&O namesake - it ran entirely on ex-PRR (and ex-MoPac) rails. Discontinued Oct 1, 1979 amid Carter-era Amtrak budget cuts and worsening Penn Central track conditions in the Midwest, leaving Columbus, Dayton, and Indianapolis without any intercity passenger rail at the time. Daily, roughly 1 round trip; frequency estimated, unsourced. (Washington branch not separately plotted here - it joined this route at Harrisburg.)", "sources": ["VERIFIED 2026-08-24: \"The National Limited was a passenger train that ran between Kansas City, Missouri, and both New York City and Washington, D.C., splitting in Pennsylvania. Amtrak operated the train from 1971 to 1979.\" The Spirit of St. Louis was renamed the National Limited in July 1971 \u2014 https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Limited_(Amtrak_train)", "Headway remains an estimate \u2014 the dates are verified, the service frequency is not."]}], "geometry_quality": "Rebuilt 2026-08-24. Philadelphia to Pittsburgh was already a dense real alignment and is unchanged (180 points). Everything west of Pittsburgh had been twelve straight lines between geocoded city centres -- Pittsburgh, Steubenville, Columbus, Dayton, Richmond, Indianapolis, Terre Haute, Effingham, St. Louis, Jefferson City, Kansas City -- averaging 110 km apart. It now reuses the corridor geometry already in this repo: pennsylvania_railroad_system.geojson#prr-panhandle-pittsburgh-st-louis for the ex-PRR Panhandle route Pittsburgh - Columbus - Indianapolis - St. Louis (543 points), and missouri_pacific_eagle.geojson#mp-missouri-river-eagle for the ex-Missouri Pacific St. Louis - Kansas City (118 points), the same physical lines the train ran on. Both joins are exact to within 0.3 km. 839 points in total, longest chord 39.5 km, down from 217 km. The Panhandle half is itself coarse in places -- 46 chords over 5 km, the worst 39 km west of Indianapolis -- so this is much better than before but not a fine trace; see the note on why a fresh OSM trace was rejected. Two small out-and-back spikes inherited from the Panhandle source (about 1 km each, near Pittsburgh and near Steubenville) were dropped; they are still present in pennsylvania_railroad_system.geojson#prr-panhandle-pittsburgh-st-louis and its Penn Central twin. 2026-08-27 CORRECTION: the Columbus - Indianapolis portion was redrawn. It previously ran on the ex-New York Central through Springfield, Piqua, Union City, Muncie and Anderson, which misses Dayton by 35 km and Richmond, Indiana by 39 km -- yet the National Limited called at Dayton Union Station, and Richmond's Pennsylvania station, which it served, closed on 1 October 1979, the day the train was discontinued. It now follows the ex-Pennsylvania Panhandle route: Columbus - Xenia - Dayton (70.5 mi) and Dayton - Richmond - Indianapolis (111.6 mi), traced on OpenStreetMap ways carrying old_railway_operator=PRR plus the Wolf Creek and Pennsy rail-trails built on the grade; 13 connectors in all, the largest 1.62 km, no straight-line approximation beyond that. The replaced section was 336 km of drawn line; the new one is 293 km. Where the new trace meets the untouched geometry there is a 1.1 km straight joint inside Columbus and a 0.2 km one at Indianapolis. Douglas-Peucker simplified at 15 m. Traced for data/richmond_indiana_rail.geojson, which carries the sources. Straight-line repair 2026-08-29: 7 ruled segments totalling 108 mi replaced with OpenStreetMap-traced rail geometry (see geometry_repairs for the OSM lines each one followed).", "geometry_repairs": [{"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-88.696231, 39.07816], "to": [-89.0076794, 38.9799333], "chord_mi": 17.86, "traced_mi": 18.04, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["CSX St. Louis Line Subdivision (18.04 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-87.0061993, 39.6080343], "to": [-87.1959042, 39.5888861], "chord_mi": 10.15, "traced_mi": 10.19, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["CSX St. Louis Line Subdivision (10.12 mi)", "(rail) (0.07 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-86.1687554, 39.7637089], "to": [-86.4842405, 39.7561896], "chord_mi": 16.74, "traced_mi": 16.78, "snap_m": [0, 0], "bridged_mi": 0.0, "osm_lines": ["CSX Indianapolis Terminal Subdivision (14.18 mi)", "CSX St. Louis Line Subdivision (2.54 mi)", "(rail) (0.05 mi)", "Indianapolis Terminal Subdivision Indianapolis Belt Branch (0.0 mi)"], "method": "Overpass rail graph, 8.0 km corridor, Dijkstra, simplified to 3.0 m"}, {"date": "2026-08-29", "from": [-85.095746, 39.812319], "to": [-85.429124, 39.801841], "chord_mi": 17.7, "traced_mi": 17.73, "snap_m": [0, 34], "bridged_mi": 1.01, "osm_lines": ["PRR [abandoned] (13.69 mi)", "National Road Heritage Trail [abandoned] (1.63 mi)", "(abandoned) [abandoned] (1.4 mi)", "(?) (1.01 mi)