94.40187, 38.96217], [-94.41352, 38.97751], [-94.41197, 39.01044], [-94.42248, 39.03528], [-94.41494, 39.06523], [-94.42122, 39.0812], [-94.4476, 39.09009], [-94.45868, 39.09942], [-94.46058, 39.10704], [-94.48337, 39.11431], [-94.55078, 39.08971], [-94.58566, 39.08657]]}}], "metadata": {"line_label_audit": "2026-08-24: line labels normalised repo-wide to \"<operating company>: <line name>\" -- the company's common display name (legal suffixes dropped), then the line as a passenger would have known it, or its two terminals where the line carried no advertised name. Long intermediate station lists were reduced to endpoints. For features spanning several operators the company shown is the one that ran the line for most of its drawn years. See CLAUDE.md Rule 6.", "sources": ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Limited_(Amtrak_train) -- Wikipedia, \"National Limited (Amtrak train)\"; lead paragraph confirms this article covers the railroad drawn in this layer. Added 2026-08-24 as a layer-level reference."], "not_yet_sourced": "Operating years 1971-1979 verified against https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Limited_(Amtrak_train). Headway is an estimate.", "geometry_rebuild": "2026-08-24. A fresh corridor-constrained OpenStreetMap trace of Pittsburgh - St. Louis was attempted first and rejected. It produced far prettier geometry (7,554 points, longest chord 2.95 km, no bridged gaps) but only 49% of its length sat on ways tagged as PRR, Penn Central or Conrail; 35% ran on New York Central rails. Weighting non-PRR ways six times heavier fixed none of it -- the path then needed 181 km of straight-line bridges and still used 234 km of NYC. The Panhandle west of Pittsburgh is largely abandoned and thinly tagged in OSM, so no faithful path exists there in the data today. Reusing this repo's own PRR Panhandle feature is coarser but is on the right railroad by construction, which matters more.", "route_correction_2026_08_27": "The Columbus - Indianapolis geometry was moved off the ex-New York Central via Muncie and onto the ex-Pennsylvania Panhandle via Dayton and Richmond, Indiana. Evidence: Amtrak took over the Pennsylvania's Columbus - Indianapolis - St. Louis train on 1 May 1971 and ran it over ex-PRR track west of Pittsburgh; Dayton Union Station is a documented stop; the Richmond, Indiana Pennsylvania station served the National Limited and closed on 1 October 1979, the day the train ended; and riders describe a 40-mile stretch from Dayton to Richmond as part of the route, matching the 42 miles traced here. Sources are listed in data/richmond_indiana_rail.geojson."}}